oindent \mbox{\em Proof.\hspace*{2mm}} Suppose $S$ does not contain any ${\Bbb F}_q$-lines. Let $H$ be any ${\Bbb F}_q$-plane in ${\Bbb P}^3$. Then $S\cap H$ is a plane curve of degree $d$ over ${\Bbb F}_q$ in $H = {\Bbb P}^2$, and has no ${\Bbb F}_q$-line as a component. Hence  egin{equation} N_q(S\cap H) \leq \left\{  egin{array}{cl} (d-1)q +1 & \mbox{m if $(d,q) 
eq (4,4)$}\ 14 & \mbox{m if $(d,q) = (4,4)$} \end{array} ight. \end{equation} by Lemma~ef{sziklai_bound}. In a term of \cite{hom2012}, defining the $s$-degree $\delta$ of $S({\Bbb F}_q)$ by \[ \delta = \max \{ N_q(S\cap H) \mid \mbox{m $H$ is an ${\Bbb F}_q$-plane} \}, \] we have \[ N_q(S) \leq (\delta -1)q + 1 + \left\lfloor  rac{\delta -1}{q +1} ightfloor \] by \cite[Proposition 2.2]{hom2012}. Hence if $(d,q) 
eq (4,4)$, then  egin{eqnarray*} N_q(S) & \leq & (d-1)q^2 +1 + \left\lfloor  rac{(d -1)q}{q +1} ightfloor\ &=& (d-1)q^2 +1 + \left\lfloor  rac{(d -2)(q+1)+q+2-d}{q +1} ightfloor\ &=& (d-1)(q^2+1) \, {m ;} \end{eqnarray*} and if $(d,q) = (4,4)$, then $N_q(S) \leq 55$. In either case, $N_q(S)$ can't be $(d-1)q^2 + dq +1.$ \hfill \mbox{$ \Box $}

With the above situation, let $H$ be an ${\Bbb F}_q$-plane of ${\Bbb P}^3$. Then either  egin{enumerate}[{m (1)}] \item $S \cap H$ is a planar ${\Bbb F}_q$-pencil of degree $\sqrt{q}+1$, or \item $S \cap H$ is a nonsingular Hermitian curve of degree $\sqrt{q}+1$ over ${\Bbb F}_q$. \end{enumerate} Furthermore, let $
u_1$ denote the number of ${\Bbb F}_q$-planes having the property {m (1)} above, and $
u_2$ the property {m (2)}. Then  egin{equation*} \left\{  egin{array}{ccc} 
u_1 &=& N_q(S) = (\sqrt{q}^3 +1)(q+1) \ 
u_2 &=& \sqrt{q}^3 (q+1)(\sqrt{q} -1). \end{array} ight. \end{equation*}

oindent \mbox{\em Last Step of the Proof of Theorem~ef{maintheorem}.\hspace*{2mm}} From Proposition~ef{sqrt_q_plus_one_case}, there is an ${\Bbb F}_q$-plane $H_{\infty} \subset {\Bbb P}^3$ such that $S \cap H_{\infty}$ is a nonsingular Hermitian curve. Choose a system of homogeneous coordinates $X_0, X_1, X_2, X_3$ of ${\Bbb P}^3$ such that  egin{enumerate}[(i)] \item $H_{\infty}$ is given by $X_0=0$; and \item the plane curve $S \cap H_{\infty}$ in $H_{\infty}={\Bbb P}^2$ is given by \[  ar{X}_1  ar{X}_2^{\sqrt{q}} +  ar{X}_1^{\sqrt{q}}  ar{X}_2 +  ar{X}_3^{\sqrt{q}+1} =0, \] where $ ar{X}_1,  ar{X}_2,  ar{X}_3$ are coordinates on $H_{\infty}$ induced by $X_1, X_2, X_3$ respectively. \end{enumerate} Let $P_1=(0,0,1,0)$ and $P_2=(0,1,0,0)$, both of which are points on $S \cap H_{\infty}$. For $\alpha = 1, 2$, the tangent line $L_{\alpha}$ at $P_{\alpha}$ to $S\cap H_{\infty}$ in $H_{\infty}={\Bbb P}^2$ is given by $ ar{X}_{\alpha}=0$. It is easy to see that $S \cap L_{\alpha}= \{ P_{\alpha} \}$. Hence for an ${\Bbb F}_q$-plane $H \supset L_{\alpha}$, \[ {}^{\#} \left( S({\Bbb F}_q) \cap (H \setminus L_{\alpha}) ight) = \left\{  egin{array}{ccl} \sqrt{q}^3 + 1 -1 & & \mbox{m if $S\cap H$ is Hermitian}\ \sqrt{q}^3 +q +1 -1& & \mbox{m if $S\cap H$ is an ${\Bbb F}_q$-pencil}. \end{array} ight. \] Counting the number $N_q(S) -1$ by using all ${\Bbb F}_q$-planes containing $L_{\alpha}$, we know that there is a unique plane $H_{\alpha, 0} \supset L_{\alpha}$ such that $S \cap H_{\alpha, 0}$ is a planar ${\Bbb F}_q$-pencil of degree $\sqrt{q}+1$; and $S \cap H_{\alpha, \lambda }$ is a nonsingular Hermitian curve for other plane $H_{\alpha, \lambda} \supset L_{\alpha}$. By changing coordinates of type \[ \left\{  egin{array}{ccc} X_1 & \mapsto & X_1 + a X_0 \ X_2 & \mapsto & X_2 + b X_0 \end{array} ight. \] if necessary, we may suppose that $H_{\alpha, 0}$ is defined by $X_{\alpha}=0$ for $\alpha =1$ and $2$ respectively. But the situation on $H_{\infty}$ never change. Summing up, $S$ is defined by \[ F(X_0, \dots , X_3) = X_0 f(X_0, \dots , X_3) + h(X_1, X_2, X_3) =0 \] where \[ h(X_1, X_2, X_3)= X_1 X_2^{\sqrt{q}} + X_1^{\sqrt{q}}X_2 + X_3^{\sqrt{q}+1} \] and $\deg f = \sqrt{q}$. Since $S \cap H_{1, 0} = S \cap \{ X_1 = 0 \}$ is a planar ${\Bbb F}_q$-pencil with the vertex $P_1= (0,0,1,0)$, \[ F(X_0, 0, X_2 , X_3) = X_0 f(X_0, 0, X_2 , X_3) + X_3^{\sqrt{q}+1} \] does not contain $X_2$, because this polynomial must have the form $ c \prod_j (X_0+\gamma_j X_3),$ with $c \in {\Bbb F}_q^{\ast}.$ Hence, in $F(X_0, X_1, X_2, X_3)$, any monomial containing $X_2$ also contains $X_1$. By the same argument on $S \cap H_{2, 0} =S \cap \{ X_2 = 0 \}$, any monomial containing $X_1$ also contains $X_2$. Therefore $f(X_0, X_1, X_2, X_3)$ is written as \[ f(X_0, X_1, X_2, X_3) = g_1(X_0, X_3) + g_2(X_0, \dots , X_3)X_1X_2, \] where $\deg g_1 = \sqrt{q}$ and $\deg g_2 = \sqrt{q}-2$. For the plane $H_{1, \lambda} = \{X_0 = \lambda X_1 \} \ (\lambda \in {\Bbb F}_q^{\ast}),$ since $S \cap \{X_0 = \lambda X_1 \}$ is a Hermitian curve,  egin{eqnarray} \lefteqn{ ho F(\lambda X_1, X_1, X_2, X_3)=}
onumber \ && ho \left( \lambda X_1 (g_1(\lambda X_1, X_3) + g_2(\lambda X_1, X_1, X_2, X_3)X_1 X_2) + h(X_1, X_2, X_3) ight) \end{eqnarray} is a Hermitian polynomial for some $ho \in {\Bbb F}_q^{\ast}$. Since $X_3^{\sqrt{q}+1}$ appears only in $h(X_1, X_2, X_3)$, the constant $ho$ must be an element of ${\Bbb F}_{\sqrt{q}}^{\ast}$. Hence (ef{polynomialinproblem}) is a Hermitian polynomial even if $ho =1$. Since $h$ itself Hermitian, \[ \lambda g_1(\lambda X_1, X_3)X_1 + \lambda g_2(\lambda X_1, X_1, X_2, X_3)X_1^2 X_2 \] must be Hermitian. Any monomial in $g_2(\lambda X_1, X_1, X_2, X_3)X_1^2 X_2$ never appear in $g_1(\lambda X_1, X_3)X_1$. So $g_2(\lambda X_1, X_1, X_2, X_3))X_1^2 X_2$ contains the monomials only of types $X_i^{\sqrt{q}+1}$ or $X_i^{\sqrt{q}}X_j$. Hence \[ g_2(\lambda X_1, X_1, X_2, X_3)X_1^2 X_2 = \mu X_1^{\sqrt{q}} X_2 \ (\mu \in {\Bbb F}_q). \] But the monomial $X_1X_2^{\sqrt{q}+1}$ can't appear in \[ g_1(\lambda X_1, X_3)X_1 + g_2(\lambda X_1, X_1, X_2, X_3)X_1^2 X_2. \] Hence $ g_2(\lambda X_1, X_1, X_2, X_3) =0$, that is, $X_0 - \lambda X_1$ is a factor of $g_2(X_0, X_1, X_2, X_3)$ for any $\lambda \in {\Bbb F}_q^{\ast}$. But $\deg g_2 = \sqrt{q}-2 \, (< q-1)$, it is impossible. Therefore $g_2(X_0, X_1, X_2, X_3) =0$ and $\lambda g_1(\lambda X_1, X_3)X_1 $ is Hermitian. Let \[ g_1(X_0, X_3) = \sum_{i=0}^{\sqrt{q}} a_i X_0^{i}X_3^{\sqrt{q}-i}. \] Then \[ \lambda g_1(\lambda X_1, X_3) X_1= \sum_{i=0}^{\sqrt{q}} a_i \lambda^{i+1} X_1^{i+1}X_3^{\sqrt{q}-i}, \] which is Hermitian. Hence $a_i = 0$ for $i 
eq 0, \, \sqrt{q}-1, \, \sqrt{q}$; and also $a_0^{\sqrt{q}} = a_{\sqrt{q}-1}$ and $a_{\sqrt{q}} \in {\Bbb F}_{\sqrt{q}}$, that is, \[ g_1(X_0, X_3) = a_0 X_3^{\sqrt{q}} + a_0^{\sqrt{q}}X_0^{\sqrt{q}-1}X_3 + a_{\sqrt{q}}X_0^{\sqrt{q}} \ \mbox{m with $a_{\sqrt{q}} \in {\Bbb F}_{\sqrt{q}}$}. \] Hence \[ F(X_0, \dots , X_3) = a_0 X_0 X_3^{\sqrt{q}} + a_0^{\sqrt{q}}X_0^{\sqrt{q}}X_3 + a_{\sqrt{q}}X_0^{\sqrt{q}+1} +h(X_1, X_2, X_3), \] which is Hermitian. If $a_{\sqrt{q}} =0$, then the surface is a cone of a nonsingular Hermitian curve with vertex $(1,0,0,0)$, and then $N_q(S) = (\sqrt{q}^3 +1)q +1$, which is not the given number. So $a_{\sqrt{q}} \in {\Bbb F}_{\sqrt{q}}^{\ast}$, and hence $S$ is a nonsingular Hermitian surface. \hfill \mbox{$ \Box $}
